(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("ibycus-fragments",[{"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "The Fragments of Ibycus"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<figure class=\"reader-figure\"><img class=\"reader-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A young kithara player, by the Goluchow painter, Athens, c. 520 BC<\/figcaption><\/figure>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "FRAGMENTS CONCERNING THE LIFE OF IBYCUS"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Eusebius Chronicle<\/span>Olympiad 61 (B.C. 536-533): — Flourished the poem-writer Ibycus."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Himerius Declamations<\/span>After the plague [or his illness] Anacreon tuned his lyre and greeted the dear Loves once more with song; after the mishap Stesichorus tuned his harp; and report hath it that Ibycus, when he fell from a chariot on the way from Catana to Himera and broke his wrist, played indeed for some time out of tune but did not dedicate his lyre to Apollo. [i. e. did not abandon his profession]"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Suidas Lexicon<\/span>Ibycus: — Son of Phytius, or, according to another account, of Polyzelus of Messene the historian; or as some writers say, of Cerdas; by birth of Rhegium. Thence he went to Samos when it was ruled by Polycrates father of the despot of that name, [or Aiaces father of the despot P.?] whose date falls in the reign of Croesus, the 54th Olympiad (B.C. 564-561). He was of an extremely amorous disposition, and was the inventor of the instrument called sambuca, which is a kind of three-cornered lyre. His works are in seven Books written in the Doric dialect. Falling one day among robbers in a deserted spot he was killed exclaiming that the very cranes which flew over at the moment would prove his avengers. Some time afterwards one of the robbers saw some cranes in the city and cried, ‘Look! the avengers of Ibycus.’ Whereupon one of the bystanders enquired into the matter of this speech of his, the crime was admitted, and the robbers brought to justice. Hence the proverb, ‘The cranes of Ibycus.’"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Statius Silvae:<br>... and Ibycus who prayed to the birds"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Garrulity<\/span>Were not the murderers of Ibycus taken as they sat in the theatre whispering with smiles together, at the sight of some cranes, that yonder were the avengers of Ibycus? For the spectators near by heard what they said, and though Ibycus had long disappeared and been mourned for dead, took up the matter of this speech and reported it to the ruling authority. Whereupon they were convicted and forthwith executed, not indeed that they were punished by the cranes, but rather compelled by their own garrulity as by some Fury or Doom-Goddess to confess to the murder they had committed. [cf. Iamb. Vit. Pythcig. 126, Suid. επιτηδευμα (above, p. 21)]"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span>On the murder of the lyrist Ibycus by robbers; by Antipater of Sidon:"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "Robbers slew thee, Ibycus, the day thou earnest to land on a desert shore untrod, but not till thou hadst called to aid thee a cloud of cranes who had come witnesses to thy woeful death. Nor was thy call in vain; for by reason of their clang an avenging Fury requited thy murder in the land of Sisyphus. [Corinth] Alas, ye greedy robbers! why fear ye not the wrath of the Gods? Even Aegisthus who slew the bard [Cassandra] in olden days escaped not the eye of the sable-robed Eumenides. [cf. Ibid. 9. 184 (vol. i. p. 2) ]"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner<\/span>The invention of this instrument (the sambuca) is ascribed by Neanthes of Cyzicus, in the 1st Book of his Annals, to Ibycus the poet of Rhegium, and that of the barbiton to Anacreon. [cf. Suid. Ίβυκανητών, E.M. ΐβις]"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Diogenian Proverbs<\/span>As ancient as Ibycus: — A proverb used of foolish persons. For Ibycus, when he might have reigned as a despot over his fellow-citizens, went away to live in Ionia. [the explanation seems to belong to the other proverb below]"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span>As foolish as Ibycus.Palatine Anthology: On the lyric poet Ibycus; anonymous:"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "I sing of the end of shoaly Italia, of Rhegium which taste th ever of the water of Sicily, because Ibycus that lover of the lyre, that lover of lads, was buried by her, his many pleasures over, beneath a leafy elm-tree, where much ivy and a bed of white reed make a covering for his grave. [cf. 9. 571]"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aristophanes Thesmophoriazusae<\/span>It is particularly unrefined of a poet to be boorish and unkempt. Just think how master-cooks of music like the great Ibycus and old Anacreon of Teos wore the cap of luxury and danced like this [i. e. in the Ionian way]."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Cicero Tusculan Disputations<\/span>What extravagant things Alcaeus writes on the love of youths! and as for Anacreon, his poetry is erotic from beginning to end. Yet to judge from his works they all were surpassed in this matter by Ibycus of Rhegium. And the love of all these poets was the sensual love."}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch On High-Birth<\/span>How often in Simonides, in Pindar, in Alcaeus, in Ibycus, in Stesichorus, is high-birth a matter of praise and honour?"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "HDR", "t": "THE POEMS OF IBYCUS"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[arranged in antiquity in 7 Books (see Suid. p. 79), to which refs, are given in only 3 passages, cf. 21, 22, 34]<\/span>"}, {"st": "1", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on love in the poets]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And Ibycus of Rhegium cries aloud:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">’Tis but in Spring the quince-trees of the Maids’ holy garden grow green with the watering rills from the river, and the vine-blossoms wax ‘neath the mantling sprays of the vines; but for me Love’s awake the year round, and like the North wind from Thrace aflame with the lightning, comes with a rush from the Cyprian, with shrivelling frenzies baleful and bold, and with masterful power shakes me to the bottom of my heart.<\/span>"}, {"st": "2", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Procl. 5. 316]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Plato Parmenides [‘I must give way’ said Parmenides; ‘and yet I feel I resemble the old race-horse about to compete in a chariot-race and trembling at the prospect because he knows what it means, to whom Ibycus likens himself when he complains that at his time of life he is made to take part in love against his will]: The words of the lyric poet Ibycus are these:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Yet again will Love eye me tenderly from beneath dark brows and cast me with manifold magic into the hopeless net of the Love-Goddess. I swear his approach makes me tremble like an old champion-horse of the chariot-race when he draws the swift car all unwillingly to the contest.<\/span>"}, {"st": "3", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Hesych. σείριον ‘Ιβυκοί πάντα]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Theon of Smyrna Explanation of Mathematical Questions Arising in Plato<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For according to Adrastus any star is called by the poets σείριος (Sirius), for instance Ibycus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">flaming like the beaming stars nightlong<\/span>"}, {"st": "4,5", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Stes. 92]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the bird called porphyrion, the purple coot]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ibycus mentions certain ‘shag-purples’ in the following lines:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">In the topmost leaves of it sit pied wild-ducks and sheeny-neckt shag-purples and wide-winged halcyons;<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">but in another passage he mentions ‘purple-birds’ simply:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Ever to me, O my heart, as a wide-winged purple-bird<\/span>"}, {"st": "6", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [on love]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Cyclops of Philoxenus of Cythera, when he praises the beauty of his love Galatea, presages his own blinding by careful avoidance of all mention of her eyes, thus: ‘O fair-faced scion of the Loves, with golden tresses and lovely voice.’ This is blind praise indeed; contrast the lines of Ibycus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Euryalus, thou scion of the delicious Graces... darling of the lovely-tressed Muses, surely thou wast the nursling of Cypris and tender-eyed Persuasion amid flowers of the rose.<\/span>"}, {"st": "7", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [on the helichryse or cassidony]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And Ibycus mentions it thus:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">myrtles and violets and cassidonies, apple-flowers and roses and glossy bay-leaves<\/span>"}, {"st": "8,9", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Il. 5. 6, Eust. Od. 1576, 56]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodian Figures of Speech<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Ibycean figure belongs both to words and to syntax, occurring in the third person subjunctive of verbs by the addition of the syllable σι... It is called Ibycean not because Ibycus was the first to use it, for it occurs in Homer before him, but because he uses it so very frequently; compare:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-q\">grey-eyed Cassandra, the lovely-tressed daughter of Priam is constrained by the talk of men,<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">and again:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">When the Dawn that ends sleep wakes the loud nightingale,<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">εγειρησι ‘wakes’ for εγειρη. [cf. Plut. Qu. Conv. 7. 3. 5, Sch. Od. 9. 364 (supporting the corruption)]<\/span>"}, {"st": "10", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Priscian Principles of Grammar<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">In this we follow the Dorians, who write for Phyleus Phyles, for Orpheus Orphes with [accusative] ‘Ορφην, for Tydeus Tydes... Similarly Ibycus writes<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Orphes of famous name<\/span>"}, {"st": "11", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum ρηγος<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— the purple coverlet; ρεξαι is used for βάφαι ‘to dye’... For ραγεις meaning ‘dyers’ and ρεγος ‘dyed cloth’ compare Anacreon... and Ibycus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">loosing their brooches and their many-coloured robes and veils<\/span>"}, {"st": "12", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Diomedes Art of Grammar [on the name]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... just as the agnomen of Ulysses is Polytlas ‘much-enduring.’ For his praenomen is, as Ibycus gives it,<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Olixes<\/span>"}, {"st": "13", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum αυσιον<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ibycus uses αίσιον ‘in vain,’ as in <i class=\"sm-qi\">for not in vain the son of Tydeus<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "14,15", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Diomedes Art of Grammar [on the name]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Patronymics... are often formed improperly from the mother, as... or from the husband, as<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-q\">Menelaid Helen,<br>or from the son, as<br>Meleagrid Althaea,<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">as we find in the Greek writer Ibycus,<\/span>"}, {"st": "16", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Cramer Inedita (Oxford). Cadmeid land: — as from Dardanus comes the patronymic in -is, Dardanis, and from Priamus Priamis, so we ought to find from Cadmus Cadmis. In the form Cadmeis therefore the e is pleonastic, and when Ibycus says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">he lay with a Cadmid maiden,<br>he uses the correct form.<\/span>"}, {"st": "17", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Galen on Hippocrates Epidemics. The word πευφιξ [the meanings seem to vary among bubble, squall or puff of wind, and ray of light, cf. Pearson Soph. Frag. 337]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">seems to have been used of a cloud, according to this line of Sophocles’ satyric drama Salmoneus: ‘... all its face with a πεμφιξ that presaged fire’; compare Ibycus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">about to drink many a πεμφιξ,<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">where the poet employs the expression according to a proverbial saying used of travellers in a storm. And thus most of the mathematicians say that the word is used of raindrops.<\/span>"}, {"st": "18,19", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodian Words without Parallel<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">No neuter ending in -ωρ, if it be of more than one syllable, has a combination of two consonants before the ω... ελδωρ ‘wish,’ for the two consonants in this word belong to separate syllables, and Ibycus sometimes uses it in a feminine form, as in this:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">not according to their wish<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">and this:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">having made known a noble wish of his<\/span>"}, {"st": "20", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Old Etymologicum, Magnum 197<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Cyaras: — Ibycus:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">nor Cyaras the general of the Medians<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Some authorities derive this from Cyaxaras by shortening, others from Cyra (?); if it comes from the compound Cyaxaras the a is not pleonastic, but if it comes from Cyra with pleonasm of a...<\/span>"}, {"st": "21", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodian Words without Parallel. τάψοσ<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... but when it is used for ‘amazement’ it is neuter; compare [Homer]: ‘She sat for a long while silent, for amazement had come to her heart.’ But perhaps this instance fails to convince. Ibycus, at any rate, has distinguished the gender in his first Book, where he follows Homer very closely:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">sat for a long time beside him fixed in amazement<\/span>"}, {"st": "22", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘I burn with love of the fair Endymion’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ibycus in his first Book makes Endymion king of Elis.<\/span>"}, {"st": "23", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Strabo Geography [on islands that have become peninsulas, etc. ]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">On the island that lies off Syracuse there is now a bridge connecting it with the mainland where there was formerly a mole, according to Ibycus, of ‘selected’ or unhewn masonry, which he calls ‘picked out’ [that is, builded].<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘Ortygia, scion of famous Syracuse’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ortygia, once an island, later became joined to the mainland; compare Ibycus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">to the dry land builded by mortal hands where once dwelt the flesh-gnawing fishes and the sea-snails<\/span>"}, {"st": "24", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Theocritus<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Arethusa: — a spring at Syracuse. It is said that the Alpheus came thither through the sea... according to Ibycus where he speaks of the Olympian cup.<\/span>"}, {"st": "25", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Dinner-table Problems [what characteristics are common to poetry and dancing]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And so he (Simonides?) has felt the fear of which the poet Ibycus says:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">I fear I may buy honour among men at the price of sin before the Gods.<\/span>"}, {"st": "26", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Aelian Natural History [on the snake called dipsas]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">I must fain charm this beast with a tale, and therefore I will not withhold one I have heard, lest. I be thought to be ignorant of it. Report hath it that Prometheus stole the fire, and this tale says that Zeus flew into a rage and gave those who told him of the theft a charm to avert old age. I understand that the recipients of this charm put it upon an ass, and the ass went on before with his pack, and growing thirsty — for it was summertime — betook himself to a spring to get him drink. But the snake that guarded that spring checked his advance, and would have driven him off had he not twisted his head about and bought his friendship with the only gift he had to hand, the charm he carried on his back. The bargain is struck. The ass drinks; the snake sloughs his old age, receiving, they say, the ass’s thirst to boot. Well now; is this tale of my own making? No, I cannot claim that for mine which was told before me by Sophocles the tragedy-writer, Deinolochus the rival of Epicharmus, Ibycus of Rhegium, and Aristeas and Apollophanes the writers of comedy.<\/span>"}, {"st": "27", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Porphyrins on the Harmonics of Ptolemaeus<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For among those who, though ignorant of music and such arts as are the subject of our present enquiry, wallow nevertheless in sophistical statements, there may well he one — I quote Ibycus —<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">There may well be one with a mouth greedy of strife who shall rouse battle against me.<\/span>"}, {"st": "28", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Chrysippus Negatives<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The poet Ibycus thus expressed himself:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">You cannot find a medicine for life when once a man is dead.<\/span>"}, {"st": "29-31", "sp": "To Gorgias", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Suid. s. Χάος]<\/span><span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Aristophanes Birds<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— ‘the void’ is here used for the air, as in Ibycus<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">and flies in a void that is strange to him.<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[perh. of the eagle which carried Ganymede to heaven; but the fr. may belong to Bacch. 5.26.]<\/span>"}, {"st": "30", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘He went through the all-fruitful garden of Zeus, and then passed out of the lofty portal of Olympus, whence there is a celestial path leading downwards; and two poles rise there, the heads of steepy mountains, summits of the earth, where the risen sun first shines red’]. In these lines he is imitating what Ibycus says in his description of the rape of Ganymede in his poem to Gorgias. Ibycus there adds how the Dawn carried off Tithonus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<figure class=\"reader-figure\"><img class=\"reader-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>“The Cranes of Ibycus” by Peter Fendi, c. 1825<\/figcaption><\/figure>"}, {"st": "31", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘Hera took her by the slender hand’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ραδινης ‘slender,’ here used to mean ‘delicate’... Ibycus speaking of the pillars that support heaven calls them ραδινοι<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">slender<br>instead of ‘very great.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "32", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">According to Ibycus, Rhadamanthus the Just was beloved by Talos.<\/span>"}, {"st": "33", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">πρόσσοθεν ‘from afar’: — the ω is shortened; compare εξοθεν ‘outside, beyond’ in Ibycus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-q\">Every reef may be safely let out so long as the sail clears the top of the wave.<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[only here is κάλως fem.]<\/span>"}, {"st": "34", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[perh. belongs to Stes. cf. him 82]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on eggs]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">In the fifth Book of his Lyric Poems Ibycus says of the Molionids:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">And the white-horsed lads the children of Molione I slew, [Heracles] like-aged, equal-headed, single-bodied, born together in a silvern egg.<\/span>"}, {"st": "35", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘and let not the daughter of Nereus put into our hands a second time the (ballot-leaves of strife’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">that is ‘leaves of contentiousness,’ or more figuratively ‘the discords of contentiousness,’ or ‘the contentions.’ Compare Ibycus:<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">the scion of Enyalius<br>and Homer: ‘branch of Ares.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "36", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘her son’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ibycus and Hesiod make Love the child of Chaos or Void.<\/span>"}, {"st": "37", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on nectar and ambrosia]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ibycus goes so far as to say that ambrosia has nine times the sweetness of honey, for he declares that honey is the ninth part of ambrosia in sweetness.<\/span>"}, {"st": "38", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Porphyrius in the Omissions declares that Hector is made the son of Apollo by Ibycus, Alexander [of Aetolia], Euphorion, and Lycophron.<\/span>"}, {"st": "39", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Same [‘As Idomeneus strode away, Deiphobus cast at him his shining javelin; for he had ever an abiding hatred of him’]: as his rival for the love of Helen, witness Ibycus and Simonides; but Idomeneus loved her, not, as Ibycus says, when he was going grey, but... [i. e. his love for her was of old and Ib. has mistaken Hom. (cf. Il. 13. 361)]<\/span>"}, {"st": "40", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Aristophanes Wasps [‘and I cannot hold my sword’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Like Menelaus; for it is said that when he went to attack Helen he dropped his sword; the story is told by Ibycus and Euripides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Euripides Andromache [‘you slew not the woman when she was in your power, but when you saw her breast you cast away your sword and received her kiss, fondling a treacherous she-dog’]: This has been better arranged by Ibycus, who makes Helen take refuge in the temple of Aphrodite and parley thence with Menelaus, who thereupon drops his sword for love of her... Details corresponding ⟨to these are given by Ibycus⟩ of Rhegium in a Dithyramb.<\/span>"}, {"st": "41", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Euripides Hecuba [‘asks that he may receive my sister Polyxena as a sweet offering and honour to his grave’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">According to Euripides and Ibycus, she was slain in sacrifice by Neoptolemus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "42", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘he shall be husband of Medea daughter of Aeetes’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... The marriage of Medea to Achilles on his arrival in the Elysian Plain is first told by Ibycus, and after him by Simonides.<\/span>"}, {"st": "43", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [‘The flaxen-haired Grey-Eyed One’ made Diomed an immortal God’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">He too was an Argive, and was immortalised for his valour. On the coast of the Adriatic there is a holy island called Diomedeia in which he is worshipped as a God; compare Ibycus... [quotation lost]<\/span>"}, {"st": "44", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘For whom alone and first and last I loosed my zone’ (i. e. my only child)]: alone, that is of the male sex; for according to Ibycus Jason had a sister Hippolyte.<\/span>"}, {"st": "45", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Ar. Ach. 392, Plat. Crat. 421 d ]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Zenobius Proverbs:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Contests allow no excuses, no more do friendships.<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The proverb-writer Milon calls this proverb Ibycean because it was first used by Ibycus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "46", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘Aetolian Leda’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ibycus calls her Leda <i class=\"sm-qi\">of Pleuron<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "47", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. Ach. Tat. in Arat. 136 Pet.]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Cramer Inedita (Oxford)<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— The Dawn-bringer and the Evening Star are the same, though in old days they were thought to be different. Recognition of their identity is first made by Ibycus of Rhegium.<\/span>"}, {"st": "48", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘Heracles... bringing the belt of war-loving Hippolyta’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">[H. carried the belt when he went on foot to Colchis] There are many stories about this belt; some call it Hippolyta’s, others Deilyca’s; Ibycus is peculiar in saying that it belonged to<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Oeolyea daughter of Briareus<\/span>"}, {"st": "49", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Aristophanes Clouds [‘Heraclean baths’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">According to Ibycus Hephaestus gave to Heracles as a gift<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">baths of warm waters<\/i> which according to some authorities is why hot springs are called Heraclean.<\/span>"}, {"st": "50", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Strabo Geography<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The orator Zoilus, in his Eulogy of Tenedos, finds fault with Homer’s accuracy as a story-writer, and declares that the Alpheus flows from Tenedos. Ibycus avers that the Sicyonian Asopus rises in Phrygia.<\/span>"}, {"st": "51", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Choeroboscus Canons<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">This is the accusative; I say that ικτίνον ‘kite’ becomes by metaplasm ικτινα... just as we find άχίτροχα<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">speeding through the sea [perh. an epithet of Asopus (50) or Alpheus (23)]<\/i> instead of αλίτροχον in Ibycus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "52", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Pausanias Description of Greece<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">They say that Sicyon was not son of Marathon son of Epopeus, but was son of Metion son of Erechtheus, and in this they have the support of Asius, while Hesiod makes him the son of Erechtheus, and Ibycus of Pelops.<\/span>"}, {"st": "53", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Philodemus On Piety<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Aeschylus in the... and Ibycus and Telestes... that the Harpies...<\/span>"}, {"st": "54", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[cf. E.M. 20. 13, Hesych. άετώσιον, E.G. 216. 26]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Sorbonicum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Herodian in the first Book of his Etymologies comes to the following conclusion: The Homeric word ετώσιος ‘fruitless’ is thought by some authorities to come from ετών ‘of years,’ but the sense forbids; others say αητών ‘of winds,’ adjective αητωσίος, with shortening αετωσιος, ‘like the wind,’<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">vain<\/i> in Ibycus, and with loss of the first syllable έτώσιον.<\/span>"}, {"st": "55,56", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Cramer Inedita (Oxford)’. The adjectival ending -ωσιος is used by the Rhegines, who regularly form it from the genitive; ‘Ανάκων ‘of the Dioscuri,’ ‘Ανακώσιοs<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">belonging to the Dioscuri<br>χαρίτων ‘of graces,’ χαριτώσιος<br>graceful<\/span>"}, {"st": "57", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Gudianum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">άτερπνος: — used by the Rhegines for άγρυπνος ‘sleepless,’ as in Ibycus and Stesichorus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "58", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Hesychius Glossary<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">βρυαλίκται: — war-dancers; compare Ibycus or Stesichorus <i class=\"sm-qi\">war-dancers stedfast in the mellay<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "59", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Old Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">διεφρασαι <i class=\"sm-qi\">thou art blind (?)<\/i><\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">in Ibycus is εφθαρσαι, by loss of θ (?) εφαρσαι, and by transposition ϊφρασχι, and so δρεφραται. Thus Herodian.<\/span>"}, {"st": "60", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Same:<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">carried off the oxen<\/i> in Ibycus; ήλσατο is for ηλάσατο.<\/span>"}, {"st": "61", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Cramer Inedita (Oxford)<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ibycus used κλαγγι<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">with noise<br>for the dative κλαγγη.<\/span>"}, {"st": "62", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodian Words without Parallel. Αιβυαφιυενης<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— the extension [of a noun] with -φι never occurs at the beginning of a compound; this happens only with an adverb, namely ιφιi ‘mightily,’ as in Iphigenia, Iphicles, Iphianassa, and the like. So Ibycus is wrong in using the word Λιβυαφιγενής<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Libya-born<\/span>"}, {"st": "63", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [‘in goat-pelts clad’]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">that is ‘skins,’ whence comes στερφωσαι ‘to cover with hide’; and Ibycus says<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">hide-clad host<br>for an army that wears skins.<\/span>"}, {"st": "64", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">τραπεζίτης with the ι has the ordinary meaning ‘hanker’ from τράπεζα ‘a table’; with the diphthong ει it means ‘one who stands beside the table,’ as in Homer ‘the dogs beside the table,’ τραπεζηες from τραπεζεύς; whereas the form with η, τραπεζίτης, used by Ibycus in the phrase<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">the dogs about the table,<br>is parallel to πληων for πλείων ‘more.’<\/span>"}, {"st": "65", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Comparison of Lycurgus and Numa<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Moreover the measures taken by Numa for the protection of virgins aim more at preserving the feminine nature and public decency than those of Lycurgus, which indeed are so entirely free and unfeminine as to have caused remark in poetry”; for the poets, for instance Ibycus, call the Spartan girls<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">bare-thighed<br>and use ‘man-mad’ of them as a term of abuse.<\/span>"}, {"st": "66", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">χάρμα is here used for χαρά ‘joy’; Homer uses it of battle; whereas in Ibycus and Stesichorus (95) it means<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">spear-head<\/span>"}, {"st": "67", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-src\">From a Papyrus of the First Century B.C.<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">[apparently part of the epilogue to a Book (or the whole collection?) of I.’s poems dedicating it to his patron Polycrates of Samos]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">... who set forth from Argos at the liest of great Zeus, and upholding an often-sung strife in tearful war for the sake of the form of flaxen-haired Helen, made an end of the city so mighty and rich and renowned of Dardanid Priam, and the vengeance of Heaven went up on long-suffering Pergamum because of the golden-tressed Cypris. But now ’tis my will to sing neither of Paris the host-cheater nor yet of slim-ankled Cassandra and other the children of Priam with the taking of Troy the high-gated, for all ’tis so glorious a theme; nor shall I recount the proud valour of the Heroes, the Heroes so noble whom the hollow ships with their nailed sides brought unto Troy for her mischief, of whom Agamemnon was chief, the Pleisthenid king, the leader of men, the son of a noble father, to wit of Atreus.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">On such tasks may the well-skilled voice of the Muses of Helicon enter, but never a mortal alive could tell of all the doings of the ships, how came Menelaus from Aulis across the Aegean Sea from Argos to Dardany nurse of horses, and how came those brazen-targeted wights, the sons of the Achaeans, of whom the most eminent in battle came swift-foot Achilles and the great and courageous Aias son of Telamon...<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-note\">[the missing strophe prob. contained a ref. to Teucer and the horses of Laomedon, which are apparently the subject of a mutilated scholion]<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">and the son of gold-girt Hyllis, aye and he to whom Troilus for loveliness of form was likened forthwith both by Trojan and Danaan even as gold thrice refined might be likened to mountain copper.<\/span>"}, {"st": "—", "sp": "N", "t": "<span class=\"sm-frag\">Theirs it is to share beauty for ever, and thine, too, Polycrates, shall be a glory, even as my glory in song, unfading.<\/span>"}],[]);})();